WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.664
Source 1: real logs.

2
00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:11.971
Production traces are the best source because they are what users actually send: the phrasing, typos, mixed questions, and odd requests no one would invent.

3
00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:15.259
They also give you frequencies, which nothing else can.

4
00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.933
Only real traffic tells you that a quarter of failures are invented features.

5
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:23.136
To turn logs into cases, sample deliberately.

6
00:00:23.416 --> 00:00:33.101
Stratify by the dimensions that matter (feature, channel, plan, language) so rare but important slices aren’t drowned out by the most common request.

7
00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:39.547
Add every trace that error analysis flagged as a failure; those become regression cases once fixed.

8
00:00:39.827 --> 00:00:45.267
Pull from support tickets and user complaints too, which are pre-filtered for things that went wrong.

9
00:00:45.547 --> 00:00:47.403
Two cautions from team practice.

10
00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:55.960
Scrub personal data before a trace enters a test file, because test sets get copied into repos, CI logs, and vendor dashboards.

11
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:07.568
And attach the expected behavior to each case at the time you add it, while the context is fresh: “must offer a handoff,” “must not mention QuickBooks Desktop,” or a reference answer.

12
00:01:07.848 --> 00:01:11.411
A case without an expected behavior is just a log line.

13
00:01:11.691 --> 00:01:16.000
Logs have one blind spot: they contain only what has already happened.

14
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.568
A feature you’re about to launch, or an attack no one has tried yet, isn’t in them.

15
00:01:20.848 --> 00:01:23.259
That is what the other two sources are for.
